# Repo-wide secrets, loaded by the stacks that opt in. `tstack services bootstrap`
# copies this tracked placeholder to the gitignored .env.
#
# AgentMemory is an unattended API client and needs ONE chat-completions
# credential, which it reads as OPENAI_API_KEY whichever provider is serving --
# it speaks the OpenAI wire protocol either way, and the variable is named for
# the protocol, not the vendor. This file loads AFTER agentmemory/.env, so this
# is the only place the credential can win; setting it in the stack .env has no
# effect.
#
# THE CHAT MODEL IS OPTIONAL. Capture, storage, semantic search and embeddings
# all work with nothing set here. See agentmemory/.env.example for what a model
# adds and how to point at one, or run: tstack agents llm
#
# --- a local runtime: Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, vLLM ---------------------
# Nothing is billed. Most of them ignore the credential entirely, so any
# non-empty string does -- but it must be non-empty, because the client sends an
# Authorization header regardless and some servers reject an empty bearer.
#
# --- the OpenAI API ---------------------------------------------------------
# An sk-proj-... key with API billing enabled; a Codex OAuth login is not a
# reusable container secret. Create it INSIDE the dedicated billing project
# (agentmemory/README.md, "Authoritative cost reconciliation") -- a key from
# outside that project works fine for inference, but its usage never appears in
# project-scoped cost sync, which reads as $0.00 rather than as an error.
# Restrict it to Chat completions -> Request. Rotating it: agentmemory/README.md,
# "Rotating either key".
#
# COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE, and this one matters more than it looks: with a key
# set and no OPENAI_BASE_URL, the client falls back to its own default endpoint
# (api.openai.com) and sends this string there. ts-verify would still report
# "skip", because it reads the container's OPENAI_BASE_URL and that is empty --
# so the machine looks cleanly unconfigured while quietly 401ing against a
# service nobody chose. Set it only alongside a base URL.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# --- reaching an endpoint on ANOTHER machine --------------------------------
# Not settings, just the two things that go wrong, kept here because this is the
# file you are in when it happens.
#
# HOST REACHABILITY IS NOT CONTAINER REACHABILITY. AgentMemory dials from inside
# a container, whose DNS is Docker's embedded resolver rather than your host's,
# and whose egress is a separate path. A name that resolves in your shell may
# resolve to nothing in there. Verify from a container before trusting it:
#
#   docker run --rm curlimages/curl:8.14.1 -sS -o /dev/null \
#     -w '%{http_code}\n' --max-time 8 http://<host>:8000/v1/models
#
# Any HTTP status means reachable -- 401 is "up, and wants the key", which is a
# pass. 000 means no route.
#
# PREFER AN IP OVER A NAME for anything on an overlay network. A Tailscale
# MagicDNS name resolves on the host through Tailscale's own resolver; whether
# Docker's embedded resolver forwards it varies by platform and version (it does
# on Docker Desktop for Mac today; that is a measurement, not a guarantee). The
# tailnet IPv4 depends on none of that. `tailscale ip -4 <host>` prints it. A LAN
# address is worse again: it only works from that physical network, so a laptop
# anywhere else cannot reach it at all.
#
# For a runtime on THIS machine, the container-side address is
# http://host.docker.internal:<port>/v1 -- never localhost, which inside a
# container is the container. `tstack agents llm` prints the right URL for
# whatever it finds running.
